{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 25, :comments ["<b>Until the death of the high priest.</b> For he comes to rest the Shekhinah upon Israel and to lengthen their days, and the murderer comes to remove the Shekhinah from Israel and to shorten the days of life — he is not worthy to be before a high priest (Sifrei); another matter — for the high priest should have prayed that this calamity not befall Israel in his lifetime (see Makkot 11)." "<b>Who anointed him with the holy oil.</b> According to its plain sense it is one of the elliptical verses of Scripture, which did not specify who anointed him, but rather like: who anointed him — the one who anoints him with the holy oil. And our Sages interpreted it in tractate Makkot (ibid.) as a proof-text, to teach that if before his trial was completed the high priest died and another was appointed in his stead, and after this his trial was completed — he goes free upon the death of the second, as it is stated <b>who anointed him</b> — but does he anoint the priest, or does the priest anoint him? Rather, to include the one anointed in his days, who releases him by his death."], :book :numbers, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T09:13:55.142154258Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}